WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.873
Security practitioners do not need quantum physics expertise to understand the migration problem.

00:00:04.873 --> 00:00:09.278
The key idea is that some quantum algorithms change the difficulty of

00:00:09.278 --> 00:00:11.481
certain mathematical problems used in cryptography.

00:00:11.481 --> 00:00:14.785
Shor's algorithm is important because, at a high level,

00:00:14.785 --> 00:00:18.456
it threatens public-key schemes based on factoring and discrete logarithms.

00:00:18.456 --> 00:00:21.760
That includes RSA, classic Diffie-Hellman, and elliptic curve systems.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.798
If a sufficiently large and reliable cryptographically relevant quantum computer existed,

00:00:25.798 --> 00:00:29.102
Shor's algorithm would undermine assumptions behind many public-key systems.

00:00:29.102 --> 00:00:33.141
That does not mean attackers can do this at scale today.

00:00:33.141 --> 00:00:35.343
It means systems being built, purchased,

00:00:35.343 --> 00:00:39.382
or archived today may still be in use when capability changes.

00:00:39.382 --> 00:00:43.787
Planning is about reducing that future exposure before the timeline becomes urgent.

00:00:43.787 --> 00:00:45.990
Grover's algorithm affects symmetric cryptography differently.

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:49.294
It can provide a quadratic speedup for brute-force search,

00:00:49.294 --> 00:00:53.699
which changes the security margin but does not collapse symmetric encryption in

00:00:53.699 --> 00:00:57.003
the same way Shor's algorithm affects many public-key schemes.

00:00:57.003 --> 00:00:57.738
In practice,

00:00:57.738 --> 00:01:02.143
this often leads to discussions about using sufficiently strong symmetric key sizes

00:01:02.143 --> 00:01:05.080
rather than redesigning every symmetric primitive from scratch.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.283
The safest practitioner posture is balanced.

00:01:07.283 --> 00:01:11.688
Avoid dismissing quantum threats because the hardest attack is not practical today.

00:01:11.688 --> 00:01:17.562
Also avoid claiming that quantum computers already break every system or make all security controls obsolete.

00:01:17.562 --> 00:01:21.967
The real work is risk-based migration: know where vulnerable public-key cryptography sits,

00:01:21.967 --> 00:01:23.803
understand data and system lifetimes,

00:01:23.803 --> 00:01:26.740
and build the ability to change cryptography deliberately.
